## Sannakji (!)

*2016 · Mingji Liu*

Mr Christopher Hulu, CEO of Kraken Industries, and a most highly distinguished giant Pacific octopus *(BCom. UC, MBA. UQ, E. dofleini)*,* *was interrupted Last Tuesday while skimming the morning paper. This was to his immense chagrin, as he had left *very specific* instructions never to be disturbed in the first five minutes of the day, for that was when he fastidiously scanned the stock prices of his Atlantic Counterparts (and also quickly checked the football scores). Christopher bubbled furiously when he was told that an external visitor (not even someone from senior management, for wrack's sake!) was waiting in his foyer and was a Would Not Take No For An Answer kind of guy. After debating whether or not to summon his personal security (consisting of two burly vampire squids who spoke very little English and were being paid off the books), Christopher decided that doing so would cause too much commotion (particularly after that nasty incident with those wretched poulpes from the union) and told his secretary to bring the visitor in. "This better be worth at least a limb", he muttered to himself while fiddling with a stack of blueprints on his desk (for Kraken Industries was a booming conglomerate, manufacturing everything from boutique fountain pens to family submersibles to over-the-counter painkillers to sucker mitts to microwaves to candleholders) and jotting down a curt Post-It note to ask H.R. about the firing policy for littoral-witted secretaries.

The visitor, to Christopher's surprise, turned out to be his old friend Dr Riley Bloop, a sharply-dressed yellow octopus *(E. zealandicus)* and Professor Emeritus of the Benthic University Environmental Science Faculty. They had first bonded over canapés five years ago at a scholarship fundraiser that Christopher's wife Ama had organised (and, if he was being entirely honest, had done a completely unremarkable job of). Since then, the two kept in semi-regular contact, exchanging Christmas cards and kelp-gardening tips to pass onto their wives. Christopher was rather fond of Dr Bloop and thought of their relationship as solid industry-university networking. "Riley, old chum!" exclaimed Christopher, "how can I be of service?" But the professor seemed nervous (and even somewhat agitated) as he floated into his office. Dr Bloop cleared his throat. "Apologies for my intrusion, Chris," he said, "but I'm afraid I come bringing dire news." His voice was like cold seeps, brimming with dark stress. "You see, I've been doing research for my upcoming paper in *The New Journal of Cephalopogenic Climatology,* one on the health of our coral reef systems. While working on it late last night, I found something deeply troubling in the data." The yellow octopus pulled a manila folder from his jacket and firmly placed it on the hardwood desk. He gave a deep sigh. "I won't mince my words, Chris. Looking at the sensor data, I've discovered that the commercial activities of Kraken Industries is putting us all in danger." He paused for effect and, upon noticing that he still held the CEO's attention, continued: "You may not know this, but I believe your factories are pumping massive quantities of toxic ink into the ocean currents. The ink emissions from your factories are killing off algae everywhere and causing irreversible coral bleaching. Huge tracts of reef from here to the Red Sea are starting to die off." Another sigh. "I'm sorry Chris, but the data is irrefutable. Your company is destroying the world as we know it."

Mr Christopher sat at his desk and listened to Dr Bloop's revelation in complete silence (except for the occasional wayward bubble). Finally, he spoke. "Tell me then, Riley. What would you have me do?" The professor's eyes immediately lit up. "Shut your factories down", he replied. "It's the only way we can save ourselves. Please Chris, I know what this company means to you and how hard you've worked to grow it, but I beg you to look into your hearts (two brachial, one systemic) and do the right thing. Here, take a look at these charts. Take my research! Read it! I want you to know that you're the first octopod I've shown this to. Because I trust you to make the right choice. And because you're my friend." A heady stillness filled the room. The giant Pacific octopus then gently nodded to his old friend and made his decision. *You know what? I think I will call security.* With the prod of a button, two vampire squids burst into the room and wrestled Dr Bloop to the ground. "Tie him up to that coffee machine over there," he carefully instructed. "Yes, yes, gag him as well." That was when Christopher also remembered the local mafia owed him a few favours. He made a quick call to a number he kept on the very last page of his personal diary and within fifteen minutes, a trio of blue-ringed octopuses had arrived to take Dr Riley Bloop away for good. Christopher requested that they destroy the research papers and send the professor up to the surface to be turned into sannakji. Sannakji was a quirky seafood dish served in Korea, where live octopods were sliced and seasoned with sesame seeds before being consumed alive, even as their arms squirmed and struggled between chomps and chews. *Hm. People are strange*, mused Christopher as he watched the mafia drag the professor into the parking lot.

The next morning, Mr Christopher Hulu, CEO of Kraken Industries, came to work, clutching his morning paper. He greeted the brand new secretary sitting outside his office and she reported that someone had called just a few moments earlier About Some Job Being Done. Content with himself, Christopher swum into his office to read for exactly five minutes. The domestic headlines were dire (as always) but Business & Finance provided adequate relief. Just as he anticipated, his Atlantic Counterparts were experiencing a momentary dive in their stock prices. But then he shook into a bubbling rage when he saw the football score. 3-0. "Bugger me," he said to no one in particular, "Spain really needs to pick up their defence."

THE END.

