## Waikato

*2016 · Mingji Liu*

The travel wiki was right: violence is general over New Zealand. Her dangerous spirit reaches even the Waikato region, here in the North Island. All the way from Coromandel to King Country, from hillock to highway, there is a slakeless brutality that rocks and rives the land.

Exhibit A. *The Waikato River.* A shock of greenblue, wide veined and livid cold, that slithers from the jaws of Ruapehu into the belly of the Tasman Sea. Be wary of its treachery! Observe how its slothful meanderings can, as suddenly as capsize, give way to strangling, rock strewn rapids.

Exhibit B. *Huka Falls.* Even the Waikato River must eventually face the mob justice of nature. As the river is forced into a narrow trench of volcanic rock, it is torturously metamorphosed and emerges a screaming cascade. A heaving roar. A thundering white. An endless storm of crystal blue.

Exhibit C. *Pohutu Geyser.* For this is what happens when the agony of the undersea can be contained no longer, when the boiling crucible beneath Te Puia explodes through pores in the earth. A jet of silica and scorching ecstasy that becomes momentarily skyborne (30m, in all) before weeping back down the geyser's ghostly white terraces.

There are scenes of violence riddled across the Waikato region. *Lake Taupo.* Swollen. Light-stabbed. *Craters of the Moon.* Smoke-haunted. Desolate. *Whakarewarewa Forest.* Blood-barked. Intemperate. Even fern and moss whisper of its lawless actions. Countless.

The travel guides are usually right. Chaos reigns this country. Hear the bellbird? See the fantail? Watch them flicker, little sufferers in the trees. They are performing an elegy for each day. Every day. All day. A new day to bring colour over the ravished, unravished earth.

